-- Example only -- renamed .sql.example so dbt does not pick it up. Shows
-- the dbt snapshot mechanic (SCD2 history, keyed on the source's own
-- updated_at), using venues purely as a syntactically plausible target --
-- **not** a recommendation to actually run this against the current data.
--
-- venues.region is conceptually the one dimension attribute where a plain
-- current-state join could misattribute historical sales if it were ever
-- corrected (see NOTES.md "Choosing which tables to snapshot" for why
-- resellers/event_type/ticket_type don't have this problem). But in this
-- generated dataset, nothing ever actually updates a venue after creation
-- -- src/generators/mutate_b2b.py only touches orders/order_items, never
-- any dimension table -- so venue_snapshot's dbt_valid_to would always be
-- NULL and dbt_valid_from would always be the first snapshot run's
-- timestamp: a real table, but one that never captures any actual history,
-- because the source it watches never changes. There is currently no table
-- in this dataset that would make a real SCD2 snapshot useful; the models
-- actually built (dbt/models/dwh/, dbt/models/datamart/) use plain
-- current-state joins throughout instead.
--
-- {% snapshot venues_snapshot %}
--
-- {{
--     config(
--       target_schema='dwh',
--       unique_key='venue_id',
--       strategy='timestamp',
--       updated_at='updated_at',
--     )
-- }}
--
-- SELECT * FROM {{ source('raw_b2b', 'venues') }}
--
-- {% endsnapshot %}
